  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nAn upper trough is centered over the Pacific Northwest with an upper \nridge centered over the southeast part of the country. South central \nand central Nebraska and north central Kansas are in between these 2 \nfeatures. A surface high is across the area behind the cold front \nthat moved through last night/early this morning. There is enhanced \natmospheric lift along and just behind the cold front which has \nresulted in the development of some showers and thunderstorms across \nportions of the area. Severe storms are not expected with higher \nstability behind the front. Additional development of showers and \nstorms is expected through the rest of the afternoon and again \ntonight. High temperatures today will generally range from the mid \n60s to mid 70s. Low temperatures tonight will mostly be in the 50s \nwith some lower 60s. \n\nThe upper ridge expands further west over the southern Plains and \nportions of the central Plains on Wednesday. This will result in \nsome portions of north central Kansas warming up into the low 80s. \nOtherwise, high temperatures will mainly be in the 70s. Showers and \nthunderstorms will again be possible (up to around 50% chance) on \nWednesday as upper lift increases over the area from shortwave \ntroughs moving overhead. Low temperatures Wednesday night will \nmainly be in the 60s with light, easterly winds. Chances of showers \nand storms continue through Wednesday night with chances up to \naround 70%. Temperatures are expected to warm up some on Thursday \nbut the amount of warming is uncertain due to model differences in \npositioning of the upper ridge and surface boundaries. Temperatures \nwill warm even further on Friday with a surface trough strengthening \nacross much of the area. Various rain and storm chances continue \nThursday and Friday as upper disturbances move over/near the area. \n\nTemperatures will cool beginning on Saturday with another cold front \nmoving into the area. Also, an upper trough from the west will move \ntowards and over the northern and central Plains through early next \nweek displacing the upper ridge that was over/near the region. There \nis some uncertainty in temperatures during the weekend and early \nnext week but most areas are expected to be in the 70s. Various rain \nand storm chances continue Saturday and Sunday with an increase in \natmospheric lift due to the cold front and approaching upper\ntrough.",
